            <ArticleTitle>Elevated serum angiopoietin-like protein 6 in women with subsequent pregnancy-induced hypertension: a preliminary study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>203-13</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/10641955.2013.784783</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Association of maternal angiopoietin-like protein 6 (Angptl6) levels with subsequent development of pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">At 24 and 32 weeks of gestation in 47 relatively overweight (BMI ≥ 24 kg/m(2)), nulliparous pregnant women serum concentrations of Angptl6 were quantified prospectively. Insulin sensitivity and lipids were measured at 24 weeks.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Angptl6 levels at 24 weeks, but not at 32 weeks, were significantly higher in women with subsequent PIH. Metabolic factors at 24 weeks did not correlate with Angptl6 levels.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This preliminary study suggests that in the second trimester, Angptl6 levels are higher in women with subsequent PIH.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
